{
  "title": "Fulfillment at Etsy",
  "subtitle": "Crafting a Shared Vision and Guiding Principles (2022)",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Why This, and Why Now?",
      "subheader": "",
      "modules": [
        {
          "id": "Why This, and Why Now?-content-1755722042513",
          "type": "text",
          "content": {
            "text": "<h3>A Gap Between Roadmaps and Strategy</h3><p>When I joined Etsy's fulfillment team, I noticed a disconnect. Teams had near-term roadmaps and delivery targets, but they lacked a unifying vision — something that tied their work to Etsy's broader business strategy. Without that north star, it was difficult to make confident decisions or understand how each initiative contributed to the bigger picture.</p><h3>Recognizing the Opportunity</h3><p>I saw an opportunity to fill that gap. Not by imposing another rigid product plan, but by creating a shared vision grounded in user research, market realities, and Etsy's brand mission. This would give teams clarity on where we wanted to evolve, while still leaving room for flexibility in execution.</p><h3>Goals of the Work</h3><p>The purpose of this effort went beyond documentation. My goals were to:</p><ul><li><strong>Create a vision</strong> that reflected user needs while aligning with Etsy's business strategy.</li><li><strong>Define guideposts</strong> that could shape yearly and quarterly planning, ensuring continuity between strategy and execution.</li><li><strong>Build consensus</strong> across design, product, engineering, and business leaders that this work was both necessary and urgent.</li></ul><h3>Leading with Influence</h3><p>Because this wasn't work that had been formally requested, success depended on influence and alignment. I initiated the effort, rallied cross-functional leadership, and created the conditions for the team to see the value in stepping back from delivery to define a shared direction.</p>"
          },
          "column": "left"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
