            "123": "o",
            "124": "n",
            "125": ".",
            "text": "<h3>What Worked Well</h3><ul><li><strong>High ownership and alignment:</strong> The workshop format empowered leaders from product, engineering, design, and operations to shape the output themselves. Because they co-authored the vision, they became natural advocates for it within their teams.</li><li><strong>Trust in a distributed setting:</strong> Facilitating in real time created stronger connections across disciplines. It deepened trust and cohesion, which was especially important given Etsy’s distributed team structure.</li><li><strong>From artifact to action:</strong> The vision and principles we created weren’t left on the shelf. They directly informed yearly planning cycles and long-term strategy discussions, proving the value of design-led alignment at the organizational level.</li></ul><h3>What I’d Improve Next Time</h3><p>Time constraints limited how far we could explore “big bets” and visual prototypes for the future vision. Preserving space for this kind of exploration would have complemented the strategic framework with tangible inspiration — showing not just <em>where</em> we were headed, but <em>what it could look like in practice.</em></p><h3>My Takeaway as a Leader</h3><p>For me, the biggest lesson was the power of creating space for co-creation. By facilitating, guiding, and amplifying the voices in the room, I shifted the work from <em>my</em> vision to <em>our</em> vision — a much stronger foundation for alignment and long-term momentum.</p>"
          },
          "column": "left"
        },
